    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "{\"role\": \"B\", \"goal\": \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", \"experiment\": \"39d748861b694c5a-h01\", \"ordinal\": 2, \"integration_source_ids\": [], \"current_source_paths\": [\"src/model-view/primetest.c\", \"src/sources/primetest.c\"], \"context_as_data\": {\"sources\": [], \"interactions\": [], \"automatic_observations\": {\"baseline\": {\"experiment_id\": \"baseline-e1\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.5, \"mean_seconds\": 0.044133203414579235}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_parent\": {\"experiment_id\": \"8cc156d46f73483d\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"Exact deterministic Miller–Rabin with early base-2 rejection and independent remaining witness lanes should eliminate the floating trial-division bottleneck on ARM64.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9025283185458559, \"mean_seconds\": 0.00476631863663594}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_base_commit\": \"d94aa41b40d7052c5de8c278987f10b5f02841b4\", \"recent_own_role\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"8cc156d46f73483d\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"Exact deterministic Miller–Rabin with early base-2 rejection and independent remaining witness lanes should eliminate the floating trial-division bottleneck on ARM64.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9025283185458559, \"mean_seconds\": 0.00476631863663594}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}], \"own_role_history\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"8cc156d46f73483d\", \"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9025283185458559, \"mean_seconds\": 0.00476631863663594}], \"node_development\": {\"attempt\": 1, \"remaining_attempts\": 3, \"previous_attempts\": [], \"confirmation_data_available\": false}}, \"documents_as_data\": [], \"deadline\": 1792315753.752171, \"generation_deadline\": 1792315753.752171, \"protocol_id\": \"a25e356eb73bf739b599f904acfc50abc67a8bc81ca09f858f820f1fb21aa617\", \"remaining_tokens\": 589824, \"official_statement_as_data\": \"Problem E\\nYet More Primes\\nPrime test is a common task in many applications, specially for security, cryptography,\\nand other interest areas. There are a number of techniques used to verify if a number is\\nprime, and the common problem is that all techniques consumes lots of time.\\nThe problem is easy to solve, giving the number to be tested and time enough to calculate.\\nBut a weird scientist took our numbers, and cut them in two halves, so we have ﬁrst to\\nre-arrange the numbers, looking for the primes. In this case, we have the certainty that all\\nthe numbers we have are primes, but the problem is how to re-order them\\nInput\\nIn the ﬁrst line, there is the numberP of prime numbers (1≤ P≤ 1000). Then, 2P lines\\nwith parts of the numbers to be tested, with no order. Prime numbers in this problem are\\ntaken as strings, so ﬁrst half and second half simply means an arbitrary cut of such string.\\nFor instance, prime number 504155039 can be cut in 5041 and 55039, or 504155 and 039\\nNote that second half numbers could begin with a lead zero, so you must take it into\\naccount. Of course, this doesn’t happen with the ﬁrst half.\\nThe input must be read from the standard input.\\nOutput\\nYou have to print the list of the P prime numbers, in ascendant order.\\nThe output must be written to the standard output.\\nExample\\nInput Output\\n4 1000213\\n50415 5041501\\n100 5575001\\n5041 504155039\\n55750 504155713\\n5039\\n01\\n0213\\n55713\\n\\n\\nThis experiment uses a reviewed finite certified input/output contract only. There are six private research cases and six distinct private confirmation cases. P is in {1,2,4,8,16,30}; the input lists two separately permuted groups of P decimal fragments. Left fragments have no leading zero and end in an even digit; right fragments have seven characters including a leading zero. Each original pair comes from a true prime and the multiset of every admissible prime concatenation equals the P source occurrences. Concatenations are at most 600971707; output is compared as exact bytes. The official example has an unresolved P/output-count contradiction. No claim is made for the whole contest domain or arbitrary fragment orders. The fixed benchmark is one member of the same certified finite set; no hidden values or expected outputs are supplied. Target: Linux ARM64, four CPU cores, 16 GiB RAM, zero GPUs. Compilation: GCC -O2 -std=c11 -Werror=uninitialized -Werror=maybe-uninitialized -lm.\", \"runtime_as_data\": {\"cpu_cores\": 4, \"OMP_NUM_THREADS\": 4, \"architecture\": \"ARM64\", \"candidate_GPU\": false, \"comparison\": \"Exact output bytes against official reference\"}, \"mother_model_as_data\": {\"phase\": \"preparation\", \"status\": \"complete\", \"guidance\": \"Research deterministic Miller–Rabin for the certified numeric range. Bases 2, 7 and 61 suffice for all unsigned 32-bit inputs and therefore cover this contract. Handle small numbers, even numbers and bases divisible by n correctly. Widen operands to uint64_t before modular multiplication; products fit safely under the stated bound. No probabilistic acceptance or floating modular arithmetic. Compare modest small-prime predivision against immediate Miller–Rabin, including all setup and process costs. Return a validated standalone program and measurements, keeping the kernel separable for integration.\", \"analysis\": \"The sole measured implementation is baseline-e1: correctness passed, score 0.5, mean 0.044133203414579235 seconds. There are no candidate timings or profiles. The source's likely dominant cost is floating remainder for successive integer divisors; repeated string concatenation and parsing are additional avoidable work. The certified contract limits enumeration to 900 concatenations and values to 600971707, making exact integer methods practical. A explores divisor-based primality, B explores deterministic modular-exponentiation primality, and C explores shared representation and filtering. Correctness means preserving every prime concatenation occurrence and emitting its sorted decimal value with exact formatting. Scope remains the certified two-group contract, not the contradictory example or unrestricted contest domain.\", \"origin\": \"model output from this fresh run; no operator analysis\"}}, \"required_json_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"hypothesis\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"transformations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"sources_kept\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"uniqueItems\": true, \"maxItems\": 0}, \"files\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"content\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"content\"]}}, \"notes\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"hypothesis\", \"transformations\", \"sources_kept\", \"files\", \"notes\"]}}\n\nCURRENT SOURCE AS DATA:\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nstatic uint32_t multiply_mod(uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t modulus)\n{\n  return (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)a * (uint64_t)b) % modulus);\n}\n\nstatic uint32_t power_two_mod(uint32_t exponent, uint32_t modulus)\n{\n  uint32_t bit = UINT32_C(1) << 31;\n  uint32_t x = 2U;\n\n  /* The exponent is positive and the modulus is odd and greater than two. */\n  while ((exponent & bit) == 0U)\n    bit >>= 1;\n  for (bit >>= 1; bit != 0U; bit >>= 1)\n    {\n      x = multiply_mod(x, x, modulus);\n      if ((exponent & bit) != 0U)\n        {\n          uint64_t doubled = (uint64_t)x + x;\n          if (doubled >= modulus)\n            doubled -= modulus;\n          x = (uint32_t)doubled;\n        }\n    }\n  return x;\n}\n\nstatic void power_mod_pair(uint32_t exponent, uint32_t modulus,\n                           uint32_t *power7, uint32_t *power61)\n{\n  uint32_t a = 7U % modulus;\n  uint32_t b = 61U % modulus;\n  uint32_t x = 1U;\n  uint32_t y = 1U;\n\n  /* A witness divisible by the modulus must be skipped. */\n  if (a == 0U)\n    a = 1U;\n  if (b == 0U)\n    b = 1U;\n\n  /* The two lanes are independent; each lane retains its true dependencies. */\n  while (exponent != 0U)\n    {\n      if ((exponent & 1U) != 0U)\n        {\n          x = multiply_mod(x, a, modulus);\n          y = multiply_mod(y, b, modulus);\n        }\n      exponent >>= 1;\n      if (exponent != 0U)\n        {\n          a = multiply_mod(a, a, modulus);\n          b = multiply_mod(b, b, modulus);\n        }\n    }\n  *power7 = x;\n  *power61 = y;\n}\n\nstatic int passes_witness(uint32_t x, uint32_t modulus, unsigned int shifts)\n{\n  unsigned int r;\n\n  if (x == 1U || x == modulus - 1U)\n    return 1;\n  for (r = 1; r < shifts; ++r)\n    {\n      x = multiply_mod(x, x, modulus);\n      if (x == modulus - 1U)\n        return 1;\n      if (x == 1U)\n        return 0;\n    }\n  return 0;\n}\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n  uint32_t n, d;\n  uint32_t power7 = 1U, power61 = 1U;\n  unsigned int shifts = 0;\n\n  if (value < 2)\n    return 0;\n  n = (uint32_t)value;\n  if ((n & 1U) == 0U)\n    return n == 2U;\n  if (n % 3U == 0U) return n == 3U;\n  if (n % 5U == 0U) return n == 5U;\n  if (n % 7U == 0U) return n == 7U;\n  if (n % 11U == 0U) return n == 11U;\n  if (n % 13U == 0U) return n == 13U;\n  if (n % 17U == 0U) return n == 17U;\n  if (n % 19U == 0U) return n == 19U;\n  if (n % 23U == 0U) return n == 23U;\n  if (n % 29U == 0U) return n == 29U;\n  if (n % 31U == 0U) return n == 31U;\n  if (n % 37U == 0U) return n == 37U;\n\n  d = n - 1U;\n  while ((d & 1U) == 0U)\n    {\n      d >>= 1;\n      ++shifts;\n    }\n\n  /* Bases 2, 7 and 61 are deterministic throughout the uint32_t range. */\n  if (!passes_witness(power_two_mod(d, n), n, shifts))\n    return 0;\n  power_mod_pair(d, n, &power7, &power61);\n  return passes_witness(power7, n, shifts) &&\n         passes_witness(power61, n, shifts);\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n         { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n              { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n          { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n            strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n            primeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n            if (isprime(primeToTest))\n               { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n               }\n          }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nstatic uint32_t multiply_mod(uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t modulus)\n{\n  return (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)a * (uint64_t)b) % modulus);\n}\n\nstatic uint32_t power_two_mod(uint32_t exponent, uint32_t modulus)\n{\n  uint32_t bit = UINT32_C(1) << 31;\n  uint32_t x = 2U;\n\n  /* The exponent is positive and the modulus is odd and greater than two. */\n  while ((exponent & bit) == 0U)\n    bit >>= 1;\n  for (bit >>= 1; bit != 0U; bit >>= 1)\n    {\n      x = multiply_mod(x, x, modulus);\n      if ((exponent & bit) != 0U)\n        {\n          uint64_t doubled = (uint64_t)x + x;\n          if (doubled >= modulus)\n            doubled -= modulus;\n          x = (uint32_t)doubled;\n        }\n    }\n  return x;\n}\n\nstatic void power_mod_pair(uint32_t exponent, uint32_t modulus,\n                           uint32_t *power7, uint32_t *power61)\n{\n  uint32_t a = 7U % modulus;\n  uint32_t b = 61U % modulus;\n  uint32_t x = 1U;\n  uint32_t y = 1U;\n\n  /* A witness divisible by the modulus must be skipped. */\n  if (a == 0U)\n    a = 1U;\n  if (b == 0U)\n    b = 1U;\n\n  /* The two lanes are independent; each lane retains its true dependencies. */\n  while (exponent != 0U)\n    {\n      if ((exponent & 1U) != 0U)\n        {\n          x = multiply_mod(x, a, modulus);\n          y = multiply_mod(y, b, modulus);\n        }\n      exponent >>= 1;\n      if (exponent != 0U)\n        {\n          a = multiply_mod(a, a, modulus);\n          b = multiply_mod(b, b, modulus);\n        }\n    }\n  *power7 = x;\n  *power61 = y;\n}\n\nstatic int passes_witness(uint32_t x, uint32_t modulus, unsigned int shifts)\n{\n  unsigned int r;\n\n  if (x == 1U || x == modulus - 1U)\n    return 1;\n  for (r = 1; r < shifts; ++r)\n    {\n      x = multiply_mod(x, x, modulus);\n      if (x == modulus - 1U)\n        return 1;\n      if (x == 1U)\n        return 0;\n    }\n  return 0;\n}\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n  uint32_t n, d;\n  uint32_t power7 = 1U, power61 = 1U;\n  unsigned int shifts = 0;\n\n  if (value < 2)\n    return 0;\n  n = (uint32_t)value;\n  if ((n & 1U) == 0U)\n    return n == 2U;\n  if (n % 3U == 0U) return n == 3U;\n  if (n % 5U == 0U) return n == 5U;\n  if (n % 7U == 0U) return n == 7U;\n  if (n % 11U == 0U) return n == 11U;\n  if (n % 13U == 0U) return n == 13U;\n  if (n % 17U == 0U) return n == 17U;\n  if (n % 19U == 0U) return n == 19U;\n  if (n % 23U == 0U) return n == 23U;\n  if (n % 29U == 0U) return n == 29U;\n  if (n % 31U == 0U) return n == 31U;\n  if (n % 37U == 0U) return n == 37U;\n\n  d = n - 1U;\n  while ((d & 1U) == 0U)\n    {\n      d >>= 1;\n      ++shifts;\n    }\n\n  /* Bases 2, 7 and 61 are deterministic throughout the uint32_t range. */\n  if (!passes_witness(power_two_mod(d, n), n, shifts))\n    return 0;\n  power_mod_pair(d, n, &power7, &power61);\n  return passes_witness(power7, n, shifts) &&\n         passes_witness(power61, n, shifts);\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n         { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n              { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n          { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n            strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n            primeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n            if (isprime(primeToTest))\n               { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n               }\n          }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n"
